Generational (nursery) garbage collector
=========================================

Goal: make ANSI-test / large loads fast by not re-marking the large
persistent live set on every collection. Tracked on the `new-gc` branch
(forked from v1.0.0).


WHY (the diagnosis behind this branch)
--------------------------------------

bocl's collector (sources/memory.c) is a non-moving mark-and-sweep with
a flat, per-zone first-fit free list. Loading the ansi-test suite is
super-linear. Measured root causes, in order of discovery:

1. Hash tables were O(n) (alist-backed)        -> fixed pre-1.0 (Slice 166, O(1) buckets).
2. large_list accumulated sub-128B split        -> fixed pre-1.0 (Slice 167, re-file to small_list).
   remainders, so allocation first-fit-walked
   past them (~80% CPU in before_best_free_block).
3. THE REMAINING ISSUE (this branch):
   * The load is ~99% garbage: one collection freed 189 of 191 MB
     (instrument garbage_collect by summing zone sizes; `live` ~ 2 MB).
   * But GC only fires when EVERY zone is full (allocate_in_zones == 0),
     so the heap balloons to ~5 GB / ~72 zones before anything is
     reclaimed; the per-allocation zone walk then scales with the zone
     count -> super-linear load (~24 min, peak RSS ~5 GB).
   * Forcing collection proactively (every ~96 MB) keeps the heap small
     (~196 MB) BUT is ~70x slower (data-and-control-flow module: 215 s
     vs 3 s) -- because the collector is NON-GENERATIONAL, so every
     collection re-marks the large persistent live set (the ~8500-line
     prelude + the ansi-test harness/`*universe*`). Frequent GC == constant
     re-marking of the old generation.

So there is a fundamental tension with the current design:
  rare GC  -> heap balloons -> slow allocation walk;
  frequent GC -> re-marks the persistent set -> slow.
The fix is to stop re-marking the old generation on every collection.


DESIGN (non-moving generational mark-sweep, "sticky mark bits")
---------------------------------------------------------------

bocl is non-moving, so we keep objects in place and track a per-object
generation rather than copying survivors out of a nursery region.

Two generations: 0 = young (nursery), 1 = old.
  * New allocations are gen-0.
  * A MINOR collection marks only gen-0 objects reachable from the roots
    AND from the remembered set; it sweeps only gen-0 garbage. gen-1
    objects are neither traced nor reclaimed -> the persistent set is not
    re-marked. Surviving gen-0 objects are promoted to gen-1 in place.
  * A MAJOR collection is the current full mark+sweep (clear all marks,
    trace everything, sweep all). Run it occasionally (e.g. when the old
    generation has grown by some factor, or every N minor GCs) to reclaim
    dead old objects and cycles between old objects.

Remembered set / write barrier (the crux):
  A gen-0 object reachable only through a gen-1 object would be missed by
  a minor GC that does not trace gen-1. So every store of a Ref into an
  ALREADY-OLD object must be recorded. Options, simplest first:
    (a) Card marking: a byte table, one entry per ~512 B card. The barrier
        marks the card of the mutated slot dirty. Minor GC scans dirty
        cards for old->young pointers. Cheap, coarse, no per-object set.
    (b) Explicit remembered set: a growable array of old objects (or slots)
        that hold young refs; barrier appends, minor GC scans + clears.
  Card marking (a) is recommended -- fewer moving parts, bounded size.

Where the barrier must live: every Ref store into a heap object --
cons_rplaca/rplacd, svref_set, structure/instance slot set, symbol
value/function/plist set, the hash-table bucket writes (hash_table.c),
vector-push, (setf gethash), array (setf aref), etc. Audit for a
chokepoint; if none exists, introduce one (a STORE_REF(obj, slot, val)
macro) and route mutations through it. This audit is step 1 and is the
main risk surface.


IMPLEMENTATION PLAN (incremental, each step validated)
------------------------------------------------------

0. Instrumentation first: land the BOCL_GCLOG hook (freed / heap / zones
   / live per collection) used in the diagnosis, plus minor/major counts.
1. Generation bit: reserve a bit in Block.objcount (currently high bit =
   mark, FREE_BLOCK = all-ones). Pick the next bit down for `old`; adjust
   mark/clear/free/objcount accessors and verify the FREE_BLOCK and
   marked_block_p invariants still hold (these are subtle -- see the
   comments in marked_block_p). Validate with BOCL_HEAPCHECK.
2. Write-barrier chokepoint: route every Ref-into-heap store through one
   macro; card table + dirty-card marking in that macro.
3. Minor collection: mark from roots + dirty cards into gen-0 only; sweep
   gen-0; promote survivors to gen-1; clear dirty cards.
4. Major collection: keep current full GC; trigger heuristically.
5. Collection policy in allocate_internal: minor GC on a young-allocation
   threshold (keeps the heap small) + major GC occasionally. Re-measure
   the ansi-test module load (target: small heap AND fast).

VALIDATION (every step)
-----------------------
* Full SACLA must-* sweep (15 files) must stay crash/hang-free and
  byte-identical to v1.0.0 (errs/fails per file unchanged).
* BOCL_GCSTRESS=N (force GC every N allocs) + BOCL_GCCHECK (survivor ->
  doomed ref check) + BOCL_HEAPCHECK (block/free-list walk) -- a
  generational barrier bug shows up here as a survivor referencing a
  swept young object. See memory note bocl-gc-rooting.
* ansi-test per-module load timing (the /tmp/modtime.lisp driver): the
  late modules (types-and-classes, strings) must drop from minutes to
  seconds while peak RSS stays bounded.

RISK: this touches the core collector + every mutation site. A missed
write barrier = a live young object swept = use-after-free, heap-state
dependent (the exact class documented in bocl-gc-rooting). Go slow; lean
on GCSTRESS/GCCHECK/HEAPCHECK after every step.


IMPLEMENTATION STATUS (v1.0.2 .. v1.0.7 on new-gc)
--------------------------------------------------
All six steps landed; correctness validated, perf goal demonstrated.

  v1.0.2  build: realpath decl + libm link order (Linux/gcc-14 prereq).
  Step 0  v1.0.3  BOCL_GCLOG (freed/live/free/heap/zones + minor/major counts).
  Step 1  v1.0.4  GC_OLD_BIT (objcount bit 30); block_objcount masks it off;
                  clear_block keeps it (sticky mark).  Byte-identical.
  Step 2  v1.0.5  Write barrier + remembered set.  gc_write_barrier(container)
                  records an OLD block on any Ref store; the target is the block
                  that PHYSICALLY holds the slot (cons cell / obj->slots Slots
                  block / vector's inner VectorOfObject).  Routed through the C
                  primitive mutators (cons_rplaca/d, svref_set, structure_set,
                  set_symbol_*) — covering all their callers — plus the direct
                  field-write bypass sites (hash table, packages, class
                  superclasses, structure cached_slots, environment push-lists,
                  reader vectors).  object_slot_count() replaces raw element-count
                  reads of objcount.  Inert until promotion exists => byte-identical.
  Step 3  v1.0.6  Minor collection + promotion model.  Promotion happens ONLY at
                  a MAJOR GC; a minor marks young (rootset + remset), reclaims
                  young garbage, promotes nothing.  Scaffold (RootSet + LivePool
                  chain) kept young; a major PINS the live-pool working set young
                  (gc_collect_pinned/demote_pinned) so a constructor mid-build is
                  never promoted out from under its initializing stores; a major
                  then rebuilds the remset by a full-heap scan (recovers the
                  promoted-wrapper -> pinned-slots edge Object_new creates).
                  Validated: SACLA byte-identical; ZERO GCCHECK(minor) dangling
                  reports under interleaved major+minor stress across must-cons /
                  -array / -hash-table / -data-and-control / -package / -symbol.
  Steps 4+5 v1.0.7  Proactive policy (opt-in: BOCL_GC_YOUNG_KB / _MAJOR_EVERY).
                  Minor GC every N KB of allocation, major every M minors.
                  Measured (120k function-call garbage, ~12 MB live):
                    legacy OFF: 74.1 s, 134 MB / 2 zones, 60 majors.
                    proactive : 26.5 s, 67 MB / 1 zone, 471 minor + 31 major.
                  ~2.8x faster AND half the peak heap.

REMAINING (blocks proactive-by-default): the pre-existing GC rooting long-tail
(issues/open/deferred-gc-rooting-longtail).  Firing the collector frequently
during a complex load (file loading / interning) hits dormant unrooted-Ref gaps
(e.g. BOCL_GCSTRESS=1 still SEGVs at startup via subclassp/typep).  Those
layout-sensitive fixes are a separate fixpoint task; once they land,
BOCL_GC_YOUNG_KB can default on and the ANSI-test module-load timings can be
re-measured end-to-end (the original target metric).


================================================================================
FOLLOW-UP (v1.0.9 / v1.0.10): rooting long-tail FIXED, proactive DEFAULTED ON,
ANSI-test load measured.
================================================================================

1. ROOTING LONG-TAIL FIXED (Slice 173, v1.0.9).  The BOCL_POISON use-after-free
   finder, run under periodic major-GC stress over every SACLA must-* file,
   pinpointed six unrooted-Ref gaps (a fresh Ref held only on the C stack -- which
   the GC does not scan -- across a call that allocates and may collect):
     - push_to_rootset (kernel.c): the global environment freed by the cons() that
       adds it to the rootset -> the subclassp/typep startup SEGV.
     - DEFMACRO (eval.c): the macro fn freed by putf() mid-plist-build.
     - read_impl (reader.c): the input stream, held only in the C-stack parser
       struct across the whole allocating parse (also fixes the intermittent
       compile-file rt.lsp hang -- see issues/open/deferred-compile-file-rt-gc-
       corruption: that "SYMBOL vs STRING" use-after-free no longer reproduces,
       5/5 clean).
     - registry_add / make_package / kernel_MAKE_PACKAGE (packages.c): the package
       name / nickname list / (name . pkg) cell freed mid-build -> dangling
       all_packages entries.
     - stream_output_string / stream_input_string (kernel.c): the backing string
       (and the fmemopen buffer) freed by string_stream_new interning STRING-STREAM.
   Validation: all 15 SACLA must-* files run clean under BOCL_POISON=1 + periodic
   major stress (zero use-after-free); normal sweep byte-identical to baseline.
   BOCL_GCSTRESS=1 no longer crashes at startup.

2. PROACTIVE DEFAULTED ON (Slice 174, v1.0.10).  BOCL_GC_YOUNG_KB now defaults to
   8192 (8 MB minor threshold); BOCL_GC_YOUNG_KB=0 restores legacy collect-when-
   full.  Full SACLA sweep under the default: all 15 files pass, counts identical
   to baseline.

3. ANSI-TEST LOAD -- the original target metric.  STATUS: the ANSI-test suite
   cannot be FUNCTIONALLY loaded by bocl yet, but NOT for any GC reason -- the
   harness needs CL features bocl has not implemented (HANDLER-CASE, ratios,
   DEFNOTE, the universe.lsp data vars, and some pathname/*load-pathname*
   machinery); the loader simply skips every form that hits a missing feature.
   The one blocker that WAS a GC bug -- compile-file of rt.lsp (gclload1's
   COMPILE-AND-LOAD "rt.lsp") hanging / SYMBOL-vs-STRING -- is fixed by item 1.
   So the GC is no longer what blocks the ANSI harness; CL-completeness is.

   GC comparison on the large loads bocl DOES execute (legacy = BOCL_GC_YOUNG_KB=0
   vs proactive default; wall-time, peak heap from BOCL_GCLOG):

     workload         legacy            proactive (8 MB)     effect
     must-reader      122.7 s / 1407 MB  30.8 s / 127 MB     3.8x faster, 11x less mem
     must-loop         45.0 s / 1279 MB  49.9 s / 191 MB     ~same time, 6.7x less mem
     must-sequence     27.5 s /   63 MB  18.4 s / 191 MB     1.5x faster
     garbage-churn     55.2 s /  639 MB 177.7 s / 319 MB     3.2x SLOWER, 1/2 mem

   Where the legacy collector balloons (must-reader/-loop: it collects almost
   never, so the heap grows to >1 GB and per-alloc work goes super-linear),
   proactive is a large win in BOTH time and memory -- exactly the issue's goal.

   TRADEOFF found: a pure high-churn allocation loop (1.5M short-lived conses) is
   ~3x SLOWER under proactive.  Legacy bump-allocates into fresh zones and never
   collects; once proactive collects, allocation comes from fragmented first-fit
   free lists, whose walk cost grows with heap size.  A threshold sweep confirms
   8 MB is the best proactive setting here (larger thresholds are worse -- 32 MB+
   time out on this loop), so the chosen default minimises the worst case while
   keeping the ballooning loads fast.  Real workloads (the full SACLA sweep) are
   unaffected; the regression is specific to synthetic allocation churn, and
   BOCL_GC_YOUNG_KB=0 restores legacy bump-allocation speed for such code.

   FUTURE WORK (not GC-rooting): the first-fit free-list allocator's super-linear
   walk on large fragmented heaps is the remaining scalability limit; a segregated
   / size-class free list (or reviving bump allocation within a zone) would remove
   the churn regression.  ANSI-test end-to-end still needs the CL-completeness
   features above before per-module timings can be taken on the real suite.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEW EVIDENCE (2026-07-12, vm branch v1.8.29, hot-6 benchmarking) — a minimal
repro of the super-linearity, with -DBOCL_PROFILE attribution, showing it now
dominates ANY allocation-heavy benchmark and poisons the process for LATER work:

  (defun lsum (n) (let ((s 0)) (dotimes (i n s) (setq s (+ s (* i i))))))
  ;; ~6 boxed-int allocations/iteration either engine (VM: 12.0M allocs at n=2M,
  ;; tree-walker: 28.0M — byte-code allocates 2.3x less than the interpreter).

  Same process, byte-compiled lsum, x86-64 Linux 16-core, image boot:
    run at n=1M   (repeated 3x): 2.6 s each — eval 1.1 s / gc 1.5 s (56%), stable.
    fresh-process n=2M:          7.5 s      — gc 69%, per-GC ~0.18 s.
    n=2M AFTER an interpreted n=2M run in the same process: 20.0 s — same 12.0M
      allocations, same 29 gc-runs, but EVAL went 2.3 s -> 12.5 s (5.4x): the
      allocator itself (charged to eval, not gc) degrades on the churned heap —
      the first-fit free-list walk after the interpreter's 28M-allocation burst.
    tests/vm-benchmark.lisp at loopsum n=2M (interp first, then VM, one process):
      interp 157 s, VM 736 s (VM "0.2x") on the normal build — i.e. at this churn
      level the numbers measure the allocator/GC, not the evaluator.  One
      pathological collection was observed at 232 s wall (a later interpreted
      200k run inheriting the heap of a VM 800k run).
  Per-GC cost also grows with heap extent: ~0.10 s (after a 1M burst) ->
  ~0.18 s (2M) -> ~0.5 s (after 28M cumulative churn).

  Consequence for benchmarking (recorded in hot-6): keep per-process allocation
  bursts below ~10M allocations or run each kernel in a fresh process; ratios
  measured above that are GC artifacts.  This strengthens the case for this
  branch (generational nursery) — the interpreter/VM work (hot-1..6, vm-1..10)
  has lowered per-operation CPU to the point where the collector is the
  dominant cost on allocation-heavy code even at moderate n.

================================================================================
NEW EVIDENCE (2026-07-18, vm branch v1.8.52, thalassa arm64) — a LIVE-SET repro
that isolates the remembered-set rescan cost (distinct from the churn repros
above, which conflate the first-fit allocator walk), and shows it inflating real
compile times as a function of resident live heap.
================================================================================

Motivation: the same-machine bocl/SBCL COMPILE-FILE ratio is not constant. It is
~64x for one modules/asdf/asdf.lisp (bocl 172-211 s vs SBCL ~3 s, small fresh
image) but ~870x for cesarum (bocl ~3600 s vs SBCL ~4.1 s, large accumulating
image). SBCL says cesarum is only ~1.4x the intrinsic work of asdf; bocl spends
~19x more. So the extra cost is not harder forms — it scales with live-heap size.

Repro (one bocl process, self-timed with GET-INTERNAL-REAL-TIME):
  A. compile asdf.lisp, fresh image                : 211 s   (RSS 76 MB)
  B. allocate 2,000,000 live rooted conses into a
     simple-vector: (dotimes (i 2000000)
                       (setf (svref *big* i) (cons i i)))  : 180 s   <-- should be <1 s
  C. recompile the SAME asdf.lisp, 2M conses (~50 MB)
     still live                                     : >24 min, NEVER FINISHED
                                                      (killed at 31 min; RSS 522 MB)
  (isolated fresh compile in its own process: 172 s, for reference.)

Two independent super-linear signals, and the array is RETAINED (almost nothing
is freed), so this is NOT the first-fit free-list walk of the churn repros — it
is the cost of scanning the growing OLD/live set on every collection:
  * B: allocating N live conses is O(N^2) (~90 us/cons at N=2M). Linear alloc
    would be milliseconds.
  * C: the identical compile is >=7x slower purely because ~50 MB of unrelated
    data is resident. Compile cost scales with resident live-heap size.

Mechanism (maps to the STATUS above, not the DESIGN):
  * Step 2 (v1.0.5) landed the remembered set as option (b) — gc_write_barrier
    records the OLD *Block* that physically holds the mutated slot — NOT the
    card marking (option a) the DESIGN section recommended. For a large
    container (a 2M-slot vector's inner VectorOfObject, or the growing
    global-env / package / method structures of a real build), the minor
    collector then rescans that ENTIRE block's slot array as roots on EVERY
    minor GC: O(slots) per minor x O(n) minors = O(n^2).
  * Additionally the MAJOR GC "rebuilds the remset by a full-heap scan"
    (v1.0.6): O(total heap) per major, so majors also get more expensive as the
    image grows.

RECOMMENDED FIX (this branch's own option (a), now clearly justified):
  * Replace the block-granular remset with CARD MARKING: a byte/bit per ~512 B
    card; the barrier dirties only the card of the mutated slot; the minor GC
    scans only DIRTY cards for old->young pointers, never the whole object.
    A 2M-slot array touched sparsely then scans a handful of cards, not 2M slots.
  * Drive the MAJOR's remset rebuild from the card table too, so no full-heap
    scan is needed to recover old->young edges.
  * The write-barrier chokepoint from Step 2 (cons_rplaca/d, svref_set,
    structure_set, set_symbol_*, + the direct-field bypass sites) already exists;
    the change is what the barrier RECORDS (dirty card, not block) and what the
    collector SCANS (dirty cards, not whole remembered blocks). Existing
    GCSTRESS/GCCHECK/HEAPCHECK validation applies unchanged — a missed card is
    the same failure class as a missed remset entry.

DISAMBIGUATION before coding (poseidon): run repro B/C under BOCL_GCLOG and
confirm the time is in minor rescans and/or the major full-heap remset rebuild
(not the free-list walk — this repro frees almost nothing, so that term should be
small). Per-GC cost should be seen to grow with heap extent, as in the 2026-07-12
section (~0.10 s -> 0.18 s -> 0.5 s).

VALIDATION target: repro B drops from O(n^2) to ~O(n) (2M conses in well under a
second), and repro C (recompile with 50 MB live) drops to near the 172-211 s
fresh-compile time. That is what turns the asdf(64x)/cesarum(870x) divergence
into a flat ~64x — the prerequisite for any large accumulating build (e.g. the
hypothetical "compile SBCL with bocl") to leave the multi-day regime.
(Measured via throwaway scratchpad drivers; not committed. See memory note
bocl-gc-superlinear.)
